WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.770 --> 00:00:04.560
- Good afternoon everyone.

00:00:04.560 --> 00:00:06.760
My name is Priya Natarajan.

00:00:06.760 --> 00:00:09.480
I'm a faculty member in the
departments of astronomy

00:00:09.480 --> 00:00:13.180
and physics at Yale and I also
serve as the current director

00:00:13.180 --> 00:00:16.580
of the university's flagship
interdisciplinary program,

00:00:16.580 --> 00:00:19.940
the Franke Program in
Science and the Humanities.

00:00:19.940 --> 00:00:23.670
I am delighted to invite
you all to today's talk

00:00:23.670 --> 00:00:27.740
by a brilliant colleague,
professor Hazel Carby.

00:00:27.740 --> 00:00:29.190
And this talk is part

00:00:29.190 --> 00:00:32.080
of the Frankie Programs
Distinguished Speaker Series.

00:00:32.080 --> 00:00:33.650
But before I introduce her,

00:00:33.650 --> 00:00:36.950
I would like to begin by first
thanking our benefactors,

00:00:36.950 --> 00:00:39.500
Mr. And Mrs. Richard and Barbara Franke,

00:00:39.500 --> 00:00:43.150
who have joined us today
and whose generosity enables

00:00:43.150 --> 00:00:45.960
this unique cross-disciplinary engagement

00:00:45.960 --> 00:00:48.520
and conversations and collaborations

00:00:48.520 --> 00:00:52.360
that transcend traditional
academic disciplinary boundaries.

00:00:52.360 --> 00:00:54.750
Also, I would like to remind all of you

00:00:54.750 --> 00:00:57.240
that as we are recording this event,

00:00:57.240 --> 00:00:58.860
I would like to request you all

00:00:58.860 --> 00:01:01.510
to mute your audio and videos.

00:01:01.510 --> 00:01:05.820
If you wish, please feel free
to start submitting questions

00:01:05.820 --> 00:01:07.581
through the Q&amp;A feature.

00:01:07.581 --> 00:01:12.581
And Hazel will take your questions
after finishing her talk.

00:01:13.440 --> 00:01:15.950
Now, to today's wonderful speaker,

00:01:15.950 --> 00:01:18.320
Hazel Carby is a Charles C.

00:01:18.320 --> 00:01:21.180
and Dorothea Dilley professor Emeritus

00:01:21.180 --> 00:01:23.930
of African-American Studies
and Professor Emeritus

00:01:23.930 --> 00:01:26.960
of American Studies at Yale University.

00:01:26.960 --> 00:01:30.160
She's also a fellow of the
Royal Society for the Arts.

00:01:30.160 --> 00:01:33.190
She's currently a visiting
Research Professor

00:01:33.190 --> 00:01:37.720
and Humanities Institute
Faculty at Dartmouth College.

00:01:37.720 --> 00:01:42.540
Her research work has focused
on race, gender, and sexuality

00:01:42.540 --> 00:01:46.090
in Caribbean and diasporic
culture and literature.

00:01:46.090 --> 00:01:48.760
And her work has centered on transnational

00:01:48.760 --> 00:01:53.030
and post-colonial literature
and theory in representations,

00:01:53.030 --> 00:01:56.580
examining the representations
of the black female body

00:01:56.580 --> 00:01:59.980
and in the genres of science fiction.

00:01:59.980 --> 00:02:04.100
Her work engages deeply
with an interrogates,

00:02:04.100 --> 00:02:06.860
the discrepancies between
the symbolic constructions

00:02:06.860 --> 00:02:08.560
of the black experience

00:02:08.560 --> 00:02:12.050
and the actual lived
lives of African-Americans

00:02:12.050 --> 00:02:15.400
a pioneering voice and
formidable intellect

00:02:15.400 --> 00:02:16.830
in black feminism,

00:02:16.830 --> 00:02:20.210
she's recognized as one of
the world's leading scholars

00:02:20.210 --> 00:02:22.040
on race and gender.

00:02:22.040 --> 00:02:24.570
One of the most influential contributions

00:02:24.570 --> 00:02:29.147
to African diaspora studies
came with her very first book,

00:02:29.147 --> 00:02:30.880
"Reconstructing Womanhood:

00:02:30.880 --> 00:02:33.530
The Emergence of the
Afro-American Woman Novelist,"

00:02:33.530 --> 00:02:35.770
published in 1987.

00:02:35.770 --> 00:02:37.300
A formidable scholar,

00:02:37.300 --> 00:02:40.670
she's the recipient of
many awards and honors.

00:02:40.670 --> 00:02:43.730
She's notably the author of
several award-winning books,

00:02:43.730 --> 00:02:47.620
including "Imperial Intimacies:
A Tale of Two Islands"

00:02:47.620 --> 00:02:50.100
published by Verso in 2019,

00:02:50.100 --> 00:02:53.390
selected as one of the
books of the year for 2019

00:02:53.390 --> 00:02:55.440
by the time the jury supplement.

00:02:55.440 --> 00:02:56.640
She's also the winner

00:02:56.640 --> 00:02:59.350
of British Academy's eighth
Nayef Al Rodhan Prize

00:02:59.350 --> 00:03:04.130
for Global Cultural Understanding
and awarded in 2020.

00:03:04.130 --> 00:03:05.730
Further recognition of her work

00:03:05.730 --> 00:03:07.650
includes her selection as a finalist

00:03:07.650 --> 00:03:12.240
for the John Hope Franklin
Publication Prize in 2020.

00:03:12.240 --> 00:03:16.050
Also in last year her
book "Imperial Intimacies"

00:03:16.050 --> 00:03:18.910
earned recognition in the
highly commended category

00:03:18.910 --> 00:03:21.140
of the PEN Hessell-Tiltman Prize.

00:03:21.140 --> 00:03:23.220
I could go on and on reading out

00:03:23.220 --> 00:03:26.680
all the wonderful achievements,
honors and awards,

00:03:26.680 --> 00:03:29.050
but without further ado,

00:03:29.050 --> 00:03:32.430
I am really excited to
hear from her today.

00:03:32.430 --> 00:03:34.070
So onto your Hazel

00:03:34.070 --> 00:03:37.940
and we are so thankful that
you accepted our invitation

00:03:37.940 --> 00:03:40.033
to speak at the Franke program.

00:03:41.880 --> 00:03:43.660
- Thank you very much Priya.

00:03:43.660 --> 00:03:46.363
I'm just going to share my screen.

00:03:48.980 --> 00:03:52.460
So thank you very much for the invitation

00:03:52.460 --> 00:03:54.550
to give this lecture.

00:03:54.550 --> 00:03:56.630
It's actually drawn from the book

00:03:56.630 --> 00:04:01.190
that you were talking about,
Priya, "Imperial Intimacies,"

00:04:01.190 --> 00:04:06.190
which I like to describe
as a very British story.

00:04:06.570 --> 00:04:10.890
It's a story of the
everyday ties relations

00:04:10.890 --> 00:04:15.110
and what I think of as
intricate interdependencies

00:04:15.110 --> 00:04:17.963
of an empire and colonialism.

00:04:19.390 --> 00:04:23.200
It does not conform to the
way in which most stories

00:04:23.200 --> 00:04:25.440
of empire have been told.

00:04:25.440 --> 00:04:28.270
And it questions what we think we know

00:04:28.270 --> 00:04:31.203
about our shared colonial past.

00:04:32.530 --> 00:04:35.470
I incorporate stories of my Welsh mother,

00:04:35.470 --> 00:04:38.383
my Jamaican father, and their ancestors,

00:04:39.320 --> 00:04:42.150
one of whom you'll hear about today

00:04:42.150 --> 00:04:47.150
into material drawn from
colonial archives and histories

00:04:47.490 --> 00:04:50.070
to show how they were shaped

00:04:50.070 --> 00:04:53.893
by the times and the
places in which they lived.

00:04:55.090 --> 00:04:59.130
But family stories and historical accounts

00:04:59.130 --> 00:05:03.273
can actually sit very
uneasily side-by-side.

00:05:04.130 --> 00:05:07.340
When I assembled the various pieces,

00:05:07.340 --> 00:05:12.340
I found that rather than
cohere into a unified narrative

00:05:13.030 --> 00:05:17.490
that juxtaposition revealed
the shards of conflict

00:05:17.490 --> 00:05:22.210
and contradiction that familial, national,

00:05:22.210 --> 00:05:26.343
and imperial ideologies work to conceal.

00:05:27.620 --> 00:05:32.620
I used family memories as a
guide to navigate material

00:05:32.680 --> 00:05:37.330
in the national archives
of Jamaica and the UK.

00:05:37.330 --> 00:05:39.500
But when I stumbled,

00:05:39.500 --> 00:05:43.840
I had to put aside the
voices of my relatives

00:05:43.840 --> 00:05:46.170
because they hindered my ability

00:05:46.170 --> 00:05:51.030
to see what they had
disguised, what they had hidden

00:05:51.030 --> 00:05:55.843
and what they had absolutely
no intention of passing on.

00:05:57.830 --> 00:06:00.760
So "Imperial Intimacies" is an account

00:06:00.760 --> 00:06:03.100
written from the perspective

00:06:03.100 --> 00:06:07.060
of someone of Jamaican Welsh ancestry

00:06:07.060 --> 00:06:12.060
who does not take for granted
definitions of being British.

00:06:12.920 --> 00:06:17.920
But rather is interested in
how subjects become British,

00:06:19.070 --> 00:06:24.070
and how people are inscribed
into ideologies of empire

00:06:24.410 --> 00:06:29.410
and beliefs of whiteness that
enable them to feel superior

00:06:30.650 --> 00:06:33.293
even when they are desperately poor.

00:06:35.520 --> 00:06:38.840
The story of imperial relations

00:06:38.840 --> 00:06:41.510
is as restless as the Atlantic

00:06:41.510 --> 00:06:45.263
searching between the islands
of Britain and Jamaica.

00:06:46.610 --> 00:06:50.746
And I follow currents that draw me back

00:06:50.746 --> 00:06:53.490
the mid 18th century.

00:06:53.490 --> 00:06:56.030
We won't be going back that far today,

00:06:56.030 --> 00:07:00.450
basically, today I'm going to
be concentrating on an area,

00:07:00.450 --> 00:07:03.220
but at the end of the 19th century

00:07:03.220 --> 00:07:05.053
in the early 20th century.

00:07:06.200 --> 00:07:11.200
But the book is also anchored
in the particular places

00:07:11.200 --> 00:07:15.360
in which people in my
family lived and died.

00:07:15.360 --> 00:07:19.210
The urban centers of
London, Brough, Bristol,

00:07:19.210 --> 00:07:21.410
Cardiff, and Kingston,

00:07:21.410 --> 00:07:24.460
and the rural landscapes of Devon,

00:07:24.460 --> 00:07:28.403
Somerset, Lincolnshire, and Portland.

00:07:29.570 --> 00:07:34.443
It's not an exceptional
story about becoming British.

00:07:35.300 --> 00:07:39.740
But instead it really
asserts that Britishness

00:07:40.960 --> 00:07:44.260
harbors the deepest interconnections

00:07:44.260 --> 00:07:47.513
of class and race and gender.

00:07:49.070 --> 00:07:51.440
Trying to find your
way as the White mother

00:07:51.440 --> 00:07:53.163
of a Black child, for example.

00:07:54.490 --> 00:07:57.900
Trying to survive as a
Black child in a colony

00:07:57.900 --> 00:07:59.933
and a Black man in the Metropol,

00:08:00.930 --> 00:08:05.780
and being a girl who was
regarded as an oxymoron

00:08:05.780 --> 00:08:10.780
when she claimed she was
both Black and British.

00:08:12.700 --> 00:08:17.053
So I'm going to begin with
this quote by Stuart hall.

00:08:18.590 --> 00:08:23.590
He said, "Identity is not
only a story, a narrative

00:08:24.397 --> 00:08:27.273
"which we tell ourselves about ourselves,

00:08:28.407 --> 00:08:33.407
"it is stories which change
with historical circumstances."

00:08:37.457 --> 00:08:41.797
"And identity shifts with
the way in which we think

00:08:41.797 --> 00:08:44.933
"and hear them and experience them.

00:08:45.987 --> 00:08:48.497
"Far from only coming from the still

00:08:48.497 --> 00:08:51.957
"small point of truth inside us,

00:08:51.957 --> 00:08:55.897
"identities actually come from outside,

00:08:55.897 --> 00:09:00.107
"they are the way in
which we are recognized

00:09:00.107 --> 00:09:05.107
"and then come to step into
the place of the recognitions

00:09:05.397 --> 00:09:07.767
"which others give us.

00:09:07.767 --> 00:09:11.797
"Without the others there is no self,

00:09:11.797 --> 00:09:15.567
"there is no self recognition."

00:09:16.840 --> 00:09:20.610
So today I'm actually
going to give an example

00:09:20.610 --> 00:09:25.610
of how in the book I use and complicate

00:09:25.720 --> 00:09:27.873
this question of identity.

00:09:28.950 --> 00:09:33.950
The example will be from Bristol
and in terms of the place.

00:09:34.420 --> 00:09:36.780
And the example of the character

00:09:36.780 --> 00:09:41.030
will be the person I know as,

00:09:41.030 --> 00:09:46.030
but also imagine as an have
created as my grandmother.

00:09:50.020 --> 00:09:54.801
So all the stories I was told by relatives

00:09:54.801 --> 00:09:59.163
under my maternal family's
identity in Bristol,

00:10:00.180 --> 00:10:02.913
the West country and Wales.

00:10:04.170 --> 00:10:06.540
But I know that my grandmother,

00:10:06.540 --> 00:10:09.270
her name was Beatrice Williams.

00:10:09.270 --> 00:10:12.720
Must've felt that she also belonged

00:10:12.720 --> 00:10:15.473
to the larger world of the British Empire.

00:10:16.911 --> 00:10:21.220
And one of my questions is how
was her sense of self shaped

00:10:22.230 --> 00:10:24.700
by her environment?

00:10:24.700 --> 00:10:28.683
And what did she know of the city's past?

00:10:30.510 --> 00:10:33.770
Bristol had been intimately in tangled

00:10:33.770 --> 00:10:36.880
with the colonial world for centuries.

00:10:36.880 --> 00:10:41.860
So how did Beatrice come
to understand herself

00:10:41.860 --> 00:10:43.960
as a British subject,

00:10:43.960 --> 00:10:47.050
who was part of an empire that ruled

00:10:47.050 --> 00:10:49.663
over a third of the globe?

00:10:52.450 --> 00:10:55.980
I lean in through time and space

00:10:55.980 --> 00:11:00.263
to tap my grandmother on the
shoulder and speak to her.

00:11:01.120 --> 00:11:03.430
As she turns towards me,

00:11:03.430 --> 00:11:06.583
I asked Beatrice if she
is like her granddaughter.

00:11:08.210 --> 00:11:12.260
If the streets of the city
are her avenue of escape

00:11:12.260 --> 00:11:14.253
as they were to the girl.

00:11:15.560 --> 00:11:20.100
I make the interests explore
every inch of her city

00:11:20.100 --> 00:11:23.113
as her granddaughter explored hers.

00:11:26.374 --> 00:11:28.830
When my grandmother had the time

00:11:28.830 --> 00:11:32.130
to leave her neighborhood of Bedminster,

00:11:32.130 --> 00:11:36.540
crossed the river Oven and
explore the city center.

00:11:36.540 --> 00:11:38.670
She would have walked up east street,

00:11:38.670 --> 00:11:41.883
crossing the new cart on
the Bedminster bridge.

00:11:43.070 --> 00:11:46.580
On her way to the Prince Street Bridge,

00:11:46.580 --> 00:11:48.700
which is an iron swing bridge

00:11:48.700 --> 00:11:51.510
that crosses the floating Harbor.

00:11:51.510 --> 00:11:54.350
She would have passed this street,

00:11:54.350 --> 00:11:56.713
Guinea street near the docks.

00:11:58.500 --> 00:12:02.713
Was Beatrice aware of the
derivation of the name Guinea?

00:12:03.910 --> 00:12:06.340
Once the site of the homes of man

00:12:06.340 --> 00:12:08.860
who traded in human flesh,

00:12:08.860 --> 00:12:12.680
the Street's name resonates
with associations,

00:12:12.680 --> 00:12:16.100
not only to the west African coast,

00:12:16.100 --> 00:12:19.270
but to the coin of exchange used

00:12:19.270 --> 00:12:21.833
by the Royal African Company.

00:12:24.750 --> 00:12:28.240
The built environment
of the center Bristol

00:12:28.240 --> 00:12:32.710
still exudes self satisfied achievement

00:12:32.710 --> 00:12:35.170
in character and appearance,

00:12:35.170 --> 00:12:37.377
the architectural grand jury

00:12:37.377 --> 00:12:40.180
and the grace of public institutions

00:12:40.180 --> 00:12:44.900
and residential squares is ample evidence

00:12:44.900 --> 00:12:47.973
of centuries of accumulated wealth.

00:12:49.550 --> 00:12:52.720
When Beatrice was a young woman,

00:12:52.720 --> 00:12:57.720
the city appeared to where
its history on it surfaces.

00:12:58.560 --> 00:13:03.190
But for centuries, Bristol's public face

00:13:03.190 --> 00:13:07.113
hit the ignominious origins of its wealth.

00:13:08.640 --> 00:13:13.640
Without exception, my maternal
relatives revered Bristol.

00:13:16.290 --> 00:13:20.340
They lived and worked
in its poorest districts

00:13:20.340 --> 00:13:23.898
but they emulated the
stilette respectability

00:13:23.898 --> 00:13:27.163
of its wealthy citizens.

00:13:28.640 --> 00:13:33.640
My grandmother's desire to
achieve a driving ambition

00:13:34.190 --> 00:13:37.600
she instilled in my mother, her daughter

00:13:37.600 --> 00:13:40.750
has its roots here in Bristol,

00:13:40.750 --> 00:13:45.750
a city that nurtured and nourished
the dreaming of strivers.

00:13:48.650 --> 00:13:53.650
Bristol had an exceptionally
robust civic culture

00:13:53.870 --> 00:13:58.870
embedded in libraries,
schools, museums, and theaters,

00:13:59.830 --> 00:14:03.490
which encouraged and confirmed ambition

00:14:03.490 --> 00:14:07.993
and profit a sense of
entitlement to its citizens.

00:14:09.540 --> 00:14:13.880
Even if Beatrice was
unable to afford tickets

00:14:13.880 --> 00:14:18.880
to actually attend a theater,
the library was free,

00:14:19.400 --> 00:14:23.000
as well lectures, lantern, slideshows,

00:14:23.000 --> 00:14:25.940
and many other public events.

00:14:25.940 --> 00:14:30.260
For a young woman whose
working life was confined

00:14:30.260 --> 00:14:35.260
to domestic labor, the wealth
of civic life offered scope

00:14:36.370 --> 00:14:38.313
for her imagination.

00:14:40.390 --> 00:14:45.390
However, unattainable the
city held promise for a future

00:14:46.320 --> 00:14:50.283
becoming modern and becoming educated.

00:14:51.640 --> 00:14:55.750
I believe that Beatrice
is fierce determination

00:14:55.750 --> 00:15:00.480
does send her own daughter to
a fine high school for girls

00:15:00.480 --> 00:15:05.480
were stimulated by the flourishing
educational institutions

00:15:05.840 --> 00:15:08.750
that she saw around her,

00:15:08.750 --> 00:15:12.330
including Colston Girls' School

00:15:12.330 --> 00:15:15.470
and here the University College of Bristol

00:15:15.470 --> 00:15:20.363
which became Bristol University in 1909.

00:15:24.360 --> 00:15:29.180
It was the city's wealthy
merchant philanthropists

00:15:29.180 --> 00:15:34.180
and large stable bourgeoisie
that had endowed Bristol

00:15:34.700 --> 00:15:39.700
with its libraries, it's
schools and universities.

00:15:39.790 --> 00:15:43.320
And with the 18th century
Georgian architecture

00:15:43.320 --> 00:15:46.960
of its monumental civic buildings,

00:15:46.960 --> 00:15:50.883
broad avenues and substantial houses.

00:15:52.320 --> 00:15:55.390
Nourished by its civic pride,

00:15:55.390 --> 00:15:59.440
Bristol made strivers of
my maternal ancestors,

00:15:59.440 --> 00:16:04.000
all of whom clung to the
ethics and the values

00:16:04.000 --> 00:16:06.223
of bushwa aspiration.

00:16:07.420 --> 00:16:12.270
If they felt a strong allegiance, in fact,

00:16:12.270 --> 00:16:14.180
to the British bourgeoisie,

00:16:14.180 --> 00:16:17.990
even though they could never ever achieve

00:16:17.990 --> 00:16:20.393
its level of financial wealth.

00:16:21.820 --> 00:16:24.770
Only when I became an adult,

00:16:24.770 --> 00:16:29.494
was I able to distinguish
between the (indistinct)

00:16:29.494 --> 00:16:32.890
and the variations of allegiance,

00:16:32.890 --> 00:16:37.600
the multiple meanings of
Bristol in the particular lives

00:16:37.600 --> 00:16:41.123
and individual pasts of my relatives.

00:16:43.870 --> 00:16:47.710
Bristol exerted symbolic power for them

00:16:47.710 --> 00:16:50.143
in its promise for the future.

00:16:51.070 --> 00:16:54.830
The possibility of progress
was the realization

00:16:54.830 --> 00:16:59.830
and fulfillment of a peculiar
form of British dreaming.

00:17:01.250 --> 00:17:04.760
And gradually I learned
to question the foundation

00:17:04.760 --> 00:17:09.340
upon which British dreams of futurity

00:17:09.340 --> 00:17:13.183
and British dreams of
belonging were built,

00:17:15.240 --> 00:17:20.240
promise flourished, because it
was grounded in and generated

00:17:20.430 --> 00:17:24.900
by the wealth and the stability visible

00:17:24.900 --> 00:17:29.120
in the bricks and mortar
of everyday Bristol life

00:17:29.120 --> 00:17:32.103
constructed by the city's merchants.

00:17:33.450 --> 00:17:37.330
The architecture clearly was testimony

00:17:37.330 --> 00:17:42.330
to British success in trade
to the ethics of hard work,

00:17:44.270 --> 00:17:49.270
the value of profit and Imperial power.

00:17:51.700 --> 00:17:55.883
Concealed was the source of that profit.

00:17:57.080 --> 00:18:01.840
The enslaved labor that
financed the city's economy

00:18:02.760 --> 00:18:05.560
from the 18th century onwards,

00:18:05.560 --> 00:18:10.083
and which paved the way
for Bristol's modernity.

00:18:11.330 --> 00:18:16.330
The enslaved labor that
enabled his Georgeann grace

00:18:16.490 --> 00:18:21.280
founded its civic
culture and made possible

00:18:21.280 --> 00:18:23.633
its philanthropic works.

00:18:25.700 --> 00:18:30.700
Sugar, tobacco, and cocoa were integral

00:18:31.353 --> 00:18:36.263
to Bristol's economy and its culture.

00:18:37.580 --> 00:18:39.850
The city was noted for its chocolate

00:18:39.850 --> 00:18:41.770
and confectionary works

00:18:41.770 --> 00:18:46.280
which alongside tobacco
factories employed large numbers

00:18:46.280 --> 00:18:48.400
of its residents.

00:18:48.400 --> 00:18:52.150
The structures that were
emblems of the city's modernity,

00:18:52.150 --> 00:18:57.150
however, physically disguised
the roots of its wealth

00:18:57.680 --> 00:19:02.393
in enslavement and continued
colonial oppression.

00:19:04.010 --> 00:19:09.010
Centuries of brutal
exploitation of African peoples

00:19:09.370 --> 00:19:14.370
enabled the philanthropy
that bought enormous benefit

00:19:15.110 --> 00:19:17.370
as well as civic grace

00:19:17.370 --> 00:19:20.853
to this English city and its citizens.

00:19:22.120 --> 00:19:25.300
The wills family, the tobacco family

00:19:25.300 --> 00:19:28.750
became abolitionists eventually.

00:19:28.750 --> 00:19:32.870
And yet they designated
the profits of enslavement

00:19:32.870 --> 00:19:34.940
to the city of Bristol

00:19:35.850 --> 00:19:40.170
not to the enslaved
who had labored to grow

00:19:40.170 --> 00:19:43.693
and harvest the tobacco they sold.

00:19:46.020 --> 00:19:50.700
The city's abolitionist
hero, Edward Colston

00:19:50.700 --> 00:19:54.290
considered Bristol's great benefactor,

00:19:54.290 --> 00:19:59.110
built schools, arms houses,
hospitals, and churches

00:19:59.110 --> 00:20:02.530
with money that he had
accrued from the trade

00:20:02.530 --> 00:20:04.950
in enslaved human beings

00:20:04.950 --> 00:20:08.250
while a member of the
Royal African Company

00:20:08.250 --> 00:20:10.753
and the society of merchant ventures,

00:20:12.370 --> 00:20:15.353
sorry, merchant ventures, I should say.

00:20:17.060 --> 00:20:20.880
Colston girls' school was recently renamed

00:20:20.880 --> 00:20:23.080
actually Montpelier High School

00:20:23.970 --> 00:20:26.780
after this statue of Edward Colston

00:20:26.780 --> 00:20:31.253
was unceremoniously dumped
in the Bristol Harbor.

00:20:34.010 --> 00:20:37.700
Bristol was deeply
bound to the enslavement

00:20:37.700 --> 00:20:41.463
and transportation of Africans
in the Atlantic trade.

00:20:42.360 --> 00:20:46.720
But it is misleading
to reduce this history

00:20:46.720 --> 00:20:51.720
to the activities of a few
wealthy individuals or traders.

00:20:54.960 --> 00:20:59.330
Beatrice and her family
passed by and went in and out

00:20:59.330 --> 00:21:03.010
of Bristol schools,
libraries, and churches,

00:21:03.010 --> 00:21:07.060
but nothing enabled them
to see the connection

00:21:07.060 --> 00:21:11.580
between trading in human
beings and Bristol's history,

00:21:11.580 --> 00:21:14.093
its wealth and civic culture.

00:21:15.220 --> 00:21:19.700
Impressed by the grand Georgian
houses of Queens Square,

00:21:19.700 --> 00:21:23.783
Royal York Crescent and Clifton.

00:21:24.790 --> 00:21:29.790
They regarded the buildings as
confirmation of achievement,

00:21:30.730 --> 00:21:35.683
the realization of ambition
and success in business.

00:21:38.680 --> 00:21:43.583
Now profits to Bristol and
its citizens were enormous,

00:21:44.630 --> 00:21:48.980
both from the export of their
goods, to the African coast,

00:21:48.980 --> 00:21:51.810
for the purchase of human beings

00:21:51.810 --> 00:21:56.593
and from the resell of human
chattel in the Caribbean.

00:21:57.930 --> 00:22:02.930
A financial outlay by
merchants of 660 pounds

00:22:07.180 --> 00:22:12.180
could reap a profit of 1000%

00:22:12.830 --> 00:22:15.383
minus the cost of transportation.

00:22:16.430 --> 00:22:21.360
A plantation owner for
an outlay of 31 pounds

00:22:21.360 --> 00:22:26.063
purchased an enormous
amount of labor power.

00:22:29.210 --> 00:22:34.210
Investment in the trade was
only one source of profits

00:22:34.510 --> 00:22:36.103
that accrued to Bristol.

00:22:37.030 --> 00:22:40.580
Residents of the city
could reap financial gain

00:22:40.580 --> 00:22:44.240
from participation in the myriad trades

00:22:44.240 --> 00:22:47.343
related to transatlantic slavery,

00:22:48.550 --> 00:22:52.040
through investment or employment,

00:22:52.040 --> 00:22:55.330
producing goods for export to Africa

00:22:55.330 --> 00:22:58.200
and the American slave colonies,

00:22:58.200 --> 00:23:00.660
servicing and Manning the ships

00:23:00.660 --> 00:23:03.903
that left Bristol on
transatlantic voyages,

00:23:04.840 --> 00:23:09.423
refining in Bristol the
products of slave labor,

00:23:10.470 --> 00:23:14.100
and handling sugar and tobacco cargoes

00:23:14.100 --> 00:23:16.113
from plantation colonies.

00:23:17.960 --> 00:23:22.700
The trade in enslaved people
was closely interwoven

00:23:22.700 --> 00:23:27.700
with the wider commercial and
economic fabric of the city.

00:23:29.400 --> 00:23:32.170
In the late 18th century,

00:23:32.170 --> 00:23:37.090
at least 40% of the income of Bostonians

00:23:37.090 --> 00:23:41.173
derived from slavery related activities.

00:23:42.400 --> 00:23:45.870
It was not only the merchants trading

00:23:45.870 --> 00:23:49.323
and enslaved human beings who profited,

00:23:50.240 --> 00:23:53.690
but barrel makers, brothel keepers,

00:23:53.690 --> 00:23:57.880
chandlers, candlemakers, carpenters,

00:23:57.880 --> 00:24:02.220
dockworkers, innkeepers, long dresses,

00:24:02.220 --> 00:24:06.323
sailors, sail makers, and shoemakers.

00:24:07.330 --> 00:24:10.670
So you get my point, everyone benefited

00:24:13.070 --> 00:24:15.980
Bristol was a city produced

00:24:15.980 --> 00:24:19.793
in circuits of exchange with Jamaica.

00:24:21.580 --> 00:24:25.980
If the history of
Bristol's financial dreams

00:24:25.980 --> 00:24:30.980
has been intimately tied to
the enslavement of others,

00:24:31.010 --> 00:24:36.010
it is also true that at
the center of this intimacy

00:24:36.320 --> 00:24:38.693
was the island of Jamaica.

00:24:39.790 --> 00:24:42.350
In 1655,

00:24:42.350 --> 00:24:47.350
all of the Cromwells seized
Jamaica from the Spanish

00:24:47.400 --> 00:24:52.400
initiating British
Imperial rule of a nation

00:24:52.520 --> 00:24:57.520
that would become one of the
world's leading sugar exporting

00:24:57.560 --> 00:24:59.750
slave driven countries

00:25:00.650 --> 00:25:04.900
and establishing the close
economic relationship

00:25:04.900 --> 00:25:07.440
between Bristol and Jamaica

00:25:07.440 --> 00:25:12.083
for sugar, tobacco and the
trade in enslaved humans.

00:25:14.470 --> 00:25:19.470
This relationship transformed and enriched

00:25:19.680 --> 00:25:23.080
both Bristol and Jamaica planters

00:25:24.110 --> 00:25:27.530
because Jamaica was the
largest sugar island

00:25:27.530 --> 00:25:29.760
in the British Caribbean,

00:25:29.760 --> 00:25:33.750
and traditionally, the
best market for slaves

00:25:33.750 --> 00:25:36.993
transported in Bristol ships.

00:25:41.600 --> 00:25:44.300
The total number of Africans

00:25:44.300 --> 00:25:48.950
who were forcibly dragged a
board British ships in chains

00:25:48.950 --> 00:25:50.530
will never be known

00:25:50.530 --> 00:25:54.293
and exact numbers are not
available for the 2,106 ships

00:25:56.810 --> 00:25:59.840
that left Bristol for the African coast.

00:25:59.840 --> 00:26:03.793
are from bear sailed with the
human cargo to the Caribbean.

00:26:04.660 --> 00:26:07.823
901 of these ships sail to Jamaica.

00:26:08.880 --> 00:26:13.880
Although final inventor is not
available for Bristol ships,

00:26:14.330 --> 00:26:19.330
historians estimate that
587,000 Africans were enslaved

00:26:21.000 --> 00:26:26.000
and that 486,000 or 82.8%
landed alive in the Americans.

00:26:30.290 --> 00:26:35.290
From the 1,591 voyages to the Caribbean,

00:26:35.410 --> 00:26:40.410
445,487 enslaved humans
were embarked onto ships

00:26:45.400 --> 00:26:50.400
and 3,700 sorry, 371,306 disembarked.

00:26:57.251 --> 00:27:02.251
217,263 of them were landed in Jamaica.

00:27:08.950 --> 00:27:12.703
I seek a reckoning with these inventories.

00:27:15.190 --> 00:27:19.810
I have to find a way to
decipher the apparently

00:27:19.810 --> 00:27:24.210
an fathomable oceanic computation

00:27:24.210 --> 00:27:28.023
in which my ancestors remain shackled.

00:27:29.990 --> 00:27:34.740
Among these anonymous
hundreds of thousands

00:27:34.740 --> 00:27:38.100
penned in black ink on graph paper,

00:27:38.100 --> 00:27:43.100
are girls and women who
survived the middle passage

00:27:43.440 --> 00:27:47.330
and are sold important Tonia Jamaica,

00:27:47.330 --> 00:27:52.143
where they will become the
property of a man called Carby.

00:27:55.170 --> 00:27:58.330
But justice Beatrice is unable

00:27:58.330 --> 00:28:02.093
to recognize the past in her present,

00:28:03.050 --> 00:28:07.850
neither can she and Raj in the
future in which her daughter

00:28:07.850 --> 00:28:11.130
would marry a descendant of this man

00:28:11.130 --> 00:28:13.383
and one of these women.

00:28:16.250 --> 00:28:19.113
As Beatrice grew from child to adult,

00:28:20.320 --> 00:28:23.150
the most pervasive ideology of modernity

00:28:24.240 --> 00:28:26.780
was the new imperialism

00:28:26.780 --> 00:28:30.273
of late Victorian and Edwardian Britain.

00:28:31.610 --> 00:28:36.610
This was a period of massive
and rapid territorial expansion

00:28:37.240 --> 00:28:40.240
for the industrial countries
of Western New York,

00:28:40.240 --> 00:28:42.850
who in competition with each other,

00:28:42.850 --> 00:28:46.210
sold new colonial acquisitions

00:28:46.210 --> 00:28:49.830
and the consolidation of that control

00:28:49.830 --> 00:28:54.830
over indigenous peoples in
Africa, Asia and the Pacific.

00:28:57.670 --> 00:28:59.970
Through image on spectacle,

00:28:59.970 --> 00:29:04.050
Beatrice would not only
have become conscious

00:29:04.050 --> 00:29:07.490
of the vast reach of the British empire,

00:29:07.490 --> 00:29:10.250
but would also have been introduced

00:29:10.250 --> 00:29:13.823
into the racial logics of whiteness.

00:29:15.950 --> 00:29:18.060
Did she become convinced

00:29:18.060 --> 00:29:21.840
of the superiority of Imperial power,

00:29:21.840 --> 00:29:25.140
and did she believe despite her poverty,

00:29:25.140 --> 00:29:30.140
that she was a member of a
superior race destined to rule

00:29:30.160 --> 00:29:32.733
over a third of the globe?

00:29:40.950 --> 00:29:44.350
So one of the most popular
forms of entertainment

00:29:44.350 --> 00:29:45.963
was the magic lantern.

00:29:47.710 --> 00:29:52.120
But lantern slide shows
were also quickly recognized

00:29:52.120 --> 00:29:55.620
for that educational potential in schools,

00:29:55.620 --> 00:29:58.360
where they were used
for teaching geography,

00:29:58.360 --> 00:30:00.283
history and science.

00:30:01.500 --> 00:30:04.140
The head teacher of Barth Road Ward School

00:30:04.140 --> 00:30:06.150
in Bristol for example,

00:30:06.150 --> 00:30:09.270
built a collection of over 2000 slides

00:30:09.270 --> 00:30:11.093
for use in the classroom.

00:30:13.290 --> 00:30:16.930
Lantern shows infinitely expanded

00:30:16.930 --> 00:30:20.270
the spatial and the geographic, sorry,

00:30:20.270 --> 00:30:25.270
the spatial and the geographical
horizons of everyday life.

00:30:27.650 --> 00:30:31.880
In 1902, a visual instruction committee

00:30:31.880 --> 00:30:36.360
was set up by the secretary
of state for the colonies

00:30:36.360 --> 00:30:40.270
to produce lantern slides on the UK

00:30:40.270 --> 00:30:44.100
for use in the territories of its empire.

00:30:44.100 --> 00:30:49.100
And five years later, the
committee embarked on a scheme

00:30:49.320 --> 00:30:54.053
to provide lecture materials
for use in British schools.

00:30:57.450 --> 00:31:00.870
But imperialism and the racialism,

00:31:00.870 --> 00:31:04.160
which was its primary characteristic

00:31:04.160 --> 00:31:08.603
existed in many forms of lantern slides.

00:31:10.040 --> 00:31:14.480
In school classrooms in
town and village halls,

00:31:14.480 --> 00:31:18.700
the magic lantern shows projected

00:31:18.700 --> 00:31:22.640
the racism and imperialism of the age,

00:31:22.640 --> 00:31:24.840
justifying the need for,

00:31:24.840 --> 00:31:28.370
and the rightness of British rule

00:31:28.370 --> 00:31:32.811
in that portrayal of the
peoples of the empire

00:31:32.811 --> 00:31:36.710
and of military exploits in colonial wars

00:31:36.710 --> 00:31:41.710
in Egypt and the Sudan,
South Africa and India.

00:31:45.780 --> 00:31:50.780
In 1897, hand tinted lantern slides

00:31:51.140 --> 00:31:54.450
of Queen Victoria's Diamond Jubilee

00:31:54.450 --> 00:31:57.583
was shown all over the country.

00:31:59.340 --> 00:32:01.930
On the initial slide
you saw at the beginning

00:32:01.930 --> 00:32:03.973
of this lecture is one of those.

00:32:05.770 --> 00:32:08.950
These shows were visual enactments

00:32:08.950 --> 00:32:13.340
and affective realizations of imperialism

00:32:13.340 --> 00:32:15.910
watching the jingoistic spectacle

00:32:15.910 --> 00:32:20.340
of colonial genuflection
to the mother of empire,

00:32:20.340 --> 00:32:23.823
generated patriotic emotion and desire.

00:32:25.070 --> 00:32:28.560
The sequences of ceremonial procession

00:32:28.560 --> 00:32:31.780
were manifestations of colonial possession

00:32:32.630 --> 00:32:35.110
and scenes of general joy,

00:32:35.110 --> 00:32:40.110
an unbounded loyalty of the
visceral pleasure of dwelling in

00:32:41.340 --> 00:32:45.543
and belonging to what was
called greater Britain.

00:32:47.830 --> 00:32:52.050
Local stores, not only invoked colonialism

00:32:52.050 --> 00:32:56.810
and their names like the British
and Colonial Meat Company,

00:32:56.810 --> 00:32:59.980
but they also signaled a shift

00:32:59.980 --> 00:33:04.330
away from dependence on
domestic agriculture,

00:33:04.330 --> 00:33:08.003
toward the increasing importation of food.

00:33:09.870 --> 00:33:13.050
The home and colonial tea stores

00:33:13.050 --> 00:33:17.290
began as a trading association in 1885.

00:33:17.290 --> 00:33:22.290
And by 1903, had over 400
retail outlets in Britain,

00:33:23.450 --> 00:33:27.950
including a branch here on
east street in Bedminster

00:33:27.950 --> 00:33:29.487
where my grandmother lived .

00:33:30.970 --> 00:33:35.450
In addition to tea, they sold
a narrow range of products,

00:33:35.450 --> 00:33:38.750
sugar, bacon, ham, margarine and butter.

00:33:38.750 --> 00:33:41.850
But increase their profit margin

00:33:41.850 --> 00:33:46.760
through ownership of their
producers and their processes

00:33:46.760 --> 00:33:49.193
overseas as well as in the UK.

00:33:50.740 --> 00:33:54.710
The shop counters had rows of compartments

00:33:54.710 --> 00:33:57.690
filled with different varieties of tea

00:33:57.690 --> 00:34:01.970
from plantations developed
in India and Sri Lanka,

00:34:01.970 --> 00:34:06.263
which was then called the
Crown Colony of Ceylon.

00:34:08.320 --> 00:34:10.403
Here is a Jubilee handkerchief.

00:34:11.810 --> 00:34:15.230
Retail stools with multiple outlets,

00:34:15.230 --> 00:34:19.200
competed for urban shoppers
through competitive pricing

00:34:19.200 --> 00:34:21.820
and long opening hours.

00:34:21.820 --> 00:34:26.100
Although they aimed to
attract the working class,

00:34:26.100 --> 00:34:30.740
Beatrice would not have
entered these stores.

00:34:30.740 --> 00:34:34.560
She could only stare through the windows,

00:34:34.560 --> 00:34:36.590
for they protected their profits

00:34:36.590 --> 00:34:41.183
by refusing to issue credit
on which the poor depended,

00:34:42.810 --> 00:34:47.580
but there was so much to
see, even from outside,

00:34:47.580 --> 00:34:52.340
since window displays
formed a rich street culture

00:34:52.340 --> 00:34:55.633
of colonial information and advertising.

00:34:57.490 --> 00:35:02.490
The companies that adopted
innovative advertising techniques

00:35:05.150 --> 00:35:08.060
with those whose goods were at the heart

00:35:08.970 --> 00:35:13.970
of the imperial economy,
tea, chocolate, soaps,

00:35:15.530 --> 00:35:20.530
and oils, tobacco and meat extracts.

00:35:23.300 --> 00:35:28.300
Biscuit tins, chocolate
wrappings, mugs and tea glass,

00:35:29.870 --> 00:35:34.870
commemorated coronations
banned the Jubilee,

00:35:36.350 --> 00:35:41.350
reproduced colonial flags
and used celebrators images

00:35:41.920 --> 00:35:44.623
of British royalty and military might.

00:35:46.750 --> 00:35:49.380
Though the family could not possibly

00:35:49.380 --> 00:35:52.040
have afforded to purchase them,

00:35:52.040 --> 00:35:56.590
commemorative mugs and
biscuit and chocolate tins

00:35:56.590 --> 00:36:01.160
would have brightened
the shells of their home

00:36:01.160 --> 00:36:03.020
in the railway cottage

00:36:04.120 --> 00:36:09.120
because they would distributed
free to school children

00:36:09.660 --> 00:36:12.060
being one of the prime a means

00:36:12.060 --> 00:36:17.060
by which school boards encouraged
patriotism in children.

00:36:21.070 --> 00:36:24.730
The windows of news agents were festooned

00:36:24.730 --> 00:36:28.350
with visual representations of the empire

00:36:28.350 --> 00:36:31.310
on which the sun never sets,

00:36:31.310 --> 00:36:36.310
including picture postcards
and juvenile papers

00:36:36.560 --> 00:36:39.440
with images of colonial adventures,

00:36:39.440 --> 00:36:43.170
which were widely circulated
through the practice

00:36:43.170 --> 00:36:46.393
of swapping individual issues.

00:36:48.150 --> 00:36:53.150
Young people were also
absolutely avid collectors

00:36:54.680 --> 00:36:56.623
of cigarette cards.

00:36:57.990 --> 00:37:02.990
On East Street sat the large
factory of WD and HO Wills,

00:37:04.300 --> 00:37:08.820
the largest manufacturer
of tobacco products,

00:37:08.820 --> 00:37:12.100
which incorporated 12 other companies

00:37:12.100 --> 00:37:16.453
to form the Imperial
Tobacco company in 1901.

00:37:19.100 --> 00:37:21.920
The cigarette cards they produced

00:37:21.920 --> 00:37:24.310
were perhaps the most influential form

00:37:24.310 --> 00:37:28.043
of collectible Imperial propaganda,

00:37:29.030 --> 00:37:32.980
combining visual and textual information

00:37:32.980 --> 00:37:35.933
with clear pedagogic intent.

00:37:38.540 --> 00:37:43.540
They were issued in series
and blank albums were sold

00:37:43.780 --> 00:37:45.843
to mount and display the cards.

00:37:47.392 --> 00:37:49.480
There were a number of cards series

00:37:49.480 --> 00:37:52.720
devoted to all aspects of empire,

00:37:52.720 --> 00:37:55.820
including arms like you see here,

00:37:55.820 --> 00:38:00.480
flags, builders of empire,
Britain's defenders,

00:38:00.480 --> 00:38:04.530
children of all nations, Indian regiments,

00:38:04.530 --> 00:38:08.000
governor generals, engineering wonders,

00:38:08.000 --> 00:38:11.023
army life and the Royal Navy.

00:38:13.280 --> 00:38:17.310
For Beatrice, as for her generation,

00:38:17.310 --> 00:38:22.310
a prerequisite of being
a modern British citizen

00:38:22.560 --> 00:38:25.313
was being an imperial subject.

00:38:26.670 --> 00:38:31.670
Visual spectacle generated and
confirmed their patriotism.

00:38:31.790 --> 00:38:36.483
Their loyalty, not only
to nation, but to empire.

00:38:38.080 --> 00:38:40.720
The aggressive expansion

00:38:40.720 --> 00:38:44.140
and the maintenance of
territorial dominance,

00:38:44.140 --> 00:38:48.330
necessitated war in far-flung regions,

00:38:48.330 --> 00:38:51.390
the significance of which was brought home

00:38:51.390 --> 00:38:55.093
through public celebrations
of military might.

00:38:56.610 --> 00:38:59.300
The return of units of the British army

00:38:59.300 --> 00:39:02.930
from the Boer War in 1902

00:39:02.930 --> 00:39:06.620
were occasions for patriotic spectacle.

00:39:06.620 --> 00:39:09.120
As they paraded through the streets

00:39:09.120 --> 00:39:12.963
of cities and towns all over the country,

00:39:14.090 --> 00:39:18.690
Mitchell and Kenyon, a
Lancashire based film company

00:39:18.690 --> 00:39:20.930
who were pioneers in the production

00:39:20.930 --> 00:39:24.440
of British motion pictures traveled south

00:39:24.440 --> 00:39:28.330
to produce a film of the
crowds gathered in Bristol

00:39:28.330 --> 00:39:32.290
to greet the return of
war hero, Lord Matthew

00:39:33.290 --> 00:39:37.103
despite of his numerous
failures on the battlefield.

00:39:38.470 --> 00:39:41.300
For Beatrice like many others,

00:39:41.300 --> 00:39:44.310
this would have been her first experience

00:39:44.310 --> 00:39:48.763
of witnessing the making
of a moving picture.

00:39:50.980 --> 00:39:53.760
Death, as you see here,

00:39:53.760 --> 00:39:58.760
also occasion spectacles
of patriotic sentiment

00:39:58.810 --> 00:40:02.090
aimed at cementing local ties

